  questions, approaches, a short spec, then a plan. Say the word if you'd rather I collapse this to a quick in-chat
  design.

  First question, the one that decides the rest.

  [1mThe fork:[0m whether the environment is chosen [3mat runtime by the code[0m or [3mat deploy time by a human/pipeline[0m. That
  determines whether the settings module contains one endpoint or a table of them, and whether it can ever be a plain
  static file you drop in place.

  - [1mRuntime detection[0m (module holds a table keyed by hostname, picks at load) — buys zero-touch deploys: same files
    everywhere, dev/staging/prod resolve themselves. Costs: prod URLs for every environment ship to every browser, and
    the hostname table is one more thing to keep current. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mDeploy-time swap[0m (module holds one endpoint; each environment gets its own copy of the file) — buys the simplest
    possible module and no environment leakage. Costs: something outside the repo has to place the right file, which you
    don't have today. Cheap to change later.
  - [1mBuild-time injection[0m (a build step substitutes the value) — buys the standard answer for real apps and keeps
    secrets/URLs out of source. Costs: introduces a toolchain this project doesn't have. Expensive to undo.

  [1mMy recommendation: runtime detection.[0m It's the only one that works with the current zero-build, open-[38;5;153mindex.html[39m setup
  without inventing deployment machinery, and the endpoint is a public API URL, so exposing the staging/prod hosts isn't
  a real leak. Build-time injection is the right call later, if this ever grows a bundler — not now.
